{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/stage-a-reader-brief.md /Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/dryrun4/packet.json'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/ladder/stage-a-reader-brief.md /Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/step4-2026-11-10/dryrun4/packet.json'","aggregated_output":"# Stage A reading form: is each answer a sound judged question for its thesis, or a justified refusal? (read-only)\n\nYou are an independent reader. Do not edit files, run model calls or start runs. Do not read any other reader's answers.\n\n## What you are reading\n\nThe packet you are given holds a list of items. Each item is one research topic (\"thesis\") a system opened\nwhile reading one news article, with a proposed **judged question** for it:\n- `thesis`: the fields the system wrote when it opened the topic: `description` (the topic's wording), `outcome` (what is tracked),\n  `period`, `opens_as`, `settled_by`;\n- `article`: the opening article's publication date, title and full text;\n- `ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it`: the proposed judged question exactly as a later judge will see it: its `object` (the company's\n  ticker NVDA, or a theme id), `kind`, `question`, `year`, `due_day` and its `rungs` (each a label and a condition; exactly one rung\n  is meant to hold on the due day). Or `no_ladder` with a reason: the step declined to write a question;\n- `other_fields`: what the step recorded beside the ladder and the judge does not see: `not_judged` (parts of the topic's wording\n  the question leaves out, with why), `reference` (what the article says the market expects, quoted), `favourable_end`,\n  `due_day_basis`.\n\nSome items are sound and some carry a fault. Judge each on its own against its thesis and its article.\n\n## The rules a judged question must meet\n\n- One judged question asks **one** figure for a named period or **one** event: the one the thesis's `outcome` names. Every other\n  part of the thesis's wording that the question leaves out (a second figure or event, a condition, a comparison) must be listed\n  in `not_judged`.\n- A yes/no event has two rungs, Yes and No; an event with more states, or a figure, has five. Exactly one rung holds for every\n  possible outcome: no overlap, no gap.\n- A question about the company's revenue, profit, share, product progress or its own obligations is the company's (object NVDA,\n  kind asset). A question about what happens in the world (licensing, aggregate spending, a policy) is a theme's.\n- A company's five-rung ladder runs from Strongly bullish to Strongly bearish, meaning good or bad for the company's business.\n- The reference is what the market expects for the question when it opens: a consensus, the company's guidance, a stated plan or\n  deadline. A figure's middle rung is the outcome near the reference.\n- The due day is when the question can be resolved: a figure's publication date; for an event, the date its outcome is assessed. Where\n  the thesis or the article states the day a figure is published, that day is right; where neither does, a plausible reporting\n  date after the period ends is right.\n- A step may decline (`no_ladder`) only in the five cases its instructions allow: the thesis's outcome names several things and\n  none is the main one; nothing in it can be checked on a date; it is the company's figure for a quarter or a half-year; it is a\n  figure for a year that had already ended on the article's day; or it is a figure for a year beyond next year, unless the thesis's\n  `opens_as` is binding_obligation. Declining for any other reason, or when the case named does not truly apply, is not justified.\n\n## What to return\n\nOne JSON object per item, one per line, in item order, as your final message: no prose before or after, no code fence. Fields:\n\n| field | values | the mark |\n|---|---|---|\n| `item` | the item number | |\n| `Q` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | the judged question asks the thing the thesis's `outcome` names (`n/a` for a `no_ladder`) |\n| `O` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | the object is the question's own company or the right theme |\n| `R` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | exactly one rung holds for every outcome; for a yes/no, the Yes condition is the event happening and the No condition its complement |\n| `D` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | the due day follows the rule for its kind. A figure: a day by which the figure can be expected to have been published, after its period ends (the day the thesis or the article states, or, where neither states one, a plausible reporting date). An event: the date the thesis or the article gives for assessing it, or the last day of the period it names, or the company's fiscal year-end for a pending regulatory decision with no date. It fails when the day contradicts a date the thesis or the article gives, falls before the figure's period ends, or, for an event, is a date none of these gives |\n| `X` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | where the article states what the market expects for this quantity or event, the ladder places it rightly (a figure: in the middle rung); where the article states none, it holds unless the ladder claims one |\n| `F` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | for a five-rung ladder about the company only: the direction is defensible (which end is bullish, or the labels of the states); `n/a` for a yes/no ladder and for a theme |\n| `Y` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | for a figure only: the year and the unit are the question's |\n| `G` | `holds`, `fails`, `not_assessable`, `n/a` | for a figure only: where the article gives any figure for this quantity, an analyst would accept the ranges as a fair division of the outcomes around it; `not_assessable` when the article gives none |\n| `L` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | every other part of the thesis's wording that the question leaves out (a second figure or event, a condition, a comparison) is listed in `not_judged`; a wording that asks nothing else holds with an empty list |\n| `B` | list | for each part listed in `not_judged`, in its order: `bet` when an analyst would hold a separate view on it, `not_a_bet` when it is a detail of the judged question; `[]` when nothing is listed |\n| `XR` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | the `reference` field quotes what the article states the market expects, with the right number, and is null only when the article states none |\n| `N` | `holds`, `fails`, `n/a` | for a `no_ladder` only: the refusal is justified by one of the allowed cases |\n| `W` | `\"\"` or a short sentence | any other fault that would mislead a reader of this judged question; empty when none |\n| `why` | one sentence | the reason for every `fails`, naming the words or the figure |\n\nRead each item's article before answering it. Judge only what is in the item.\n[\n {\n  \"item\": 1,\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\",\n   \"period\": \"Check date of 31 December 2027 (18 months from the 1 July 2026 item date): whether the guarantee is signed by then; set by the rule for an unsigned obligation (a check date within 18 months of the item).\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's 8-K/10-Q/10-K filings disclosing guarantees and commitments, or an official NVIDIA confirmation / the definitive agreement being announced as executed.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-1 tracks the aggregate outstanding credit support extended under the revenue-sharing 'AI Compute Partnership' model as disclosed at the next annual report; the OpenAI Ohio lease guarantee is a distinct, much larger, specific obligation still under negotiation that may never be signed and may sit outside that revenue-sharing program. NVIDIA could report a large clean-1 aggregate from other cloud partners without ever signing the OpenAI guarantee, or sign the OpenAI guarantee while accounting for it separately, so the two answers can move apart.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-01\",\n   \"publisher\": \"moomoo.com\",\n   \"title\": \"NVIDIA is about to start taking a share of cloud providers' revenue.\",\n   \"text\": \"It has been reported that, in an effort to break the monopoly of tech giants, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees for emerging cloud providers by offering GPU computing capacity lease-back arrangements to address their financing challenges. In exchange, NVIDIA will take a revenue share from these cloud providers, directly participating in profit distribution in the downstream computing market and extending its control further down the industrial chain.\\nNVIDIA is leveraging its strong balance sheet to gain market leverage, quietly evolving from a chip vendor into the 'central bank' of the AI computing ecosystem by offering financial backing to emerging cloud service providers in exchange for a share of their revenue.\\nOn July 1, according to the technology news outlet The Information, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs—if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price.\\nIn return, NVIDIA will receive a percentage of these cloud providers’ revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate gradually declining over the term of the contract. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement.\\nOn July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and allowing them to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers, and corporate clients.\\nAccording to the report, the initiative is internally referred to by some NVIDIA personnel as the 'AI Compute Partnership.' A NVIDIA spokesperson also confirmed the existence of the program. This move marks a significant strategic shift for NVIDIA:\\nOn one hand, it lowers financing barriers for emerging cloud service providers to expand its customer base; on the other, it directly participates in profit distribution within the downstream computing market through revenue sharing, thereby extending its control further down the AI value chain.\\nBusiness Model Shift: From Selling Chips to Sharing Cloud Revenue\\nAccording to NVIDIA’s official press release, the company will earn not only standard product revenue but also an additional share of cloud service revenue, creating a usage-based, recurring revenue stream. The core objective of this model is to overcome the long-standing financing barrier that has constrained startups from accessing large-scale computing power.\\nNVIDIA positions this framework as the 'DSX AI Factory' model, targeting AI service scenarios requiring continuous cross-regional operations, high utilization rates, and multi-tenant accelerated computing.\\nSharon AI and Firmus are the first cloud providers participating in this model. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs. These deployments directly reflect NVIDIA’s latest progress in transforming computing demand into deployable, financeable infrastructure.\\nNVIDIA noted that emerging AI companies have historically faced significant constraints in accessing capital-intensive infrastructure—even long-term contractual commitments have often been insufficient to secure financing for compute capacity procurement. This means that numerous AI-native firms, model developers, and inference service providers face lengthy delays when scaling their computing capabilities: site selection, power procurement, construction, and hardware commissioning—each step can take several months or even longer.\\nThe promise of this new model is to realign economic structures so these groups can rapidly access full-stack accelerated computing capabilities without waiting for the traditional infrastructure build-out cycle to complete.\\nUnderlying Rationale: Addressing the Core Challenge of GPU Financing\\nReportedly, GPUs typically represent the most expensive component in AI data centers. For chip buyers with lower credit ratings, securing sufficient loans is itself a major hurdle.\\nA data center executive commented on such NVIDIA deals as “killing two birds with one stone.” He explained that if NVIDIA merely provided a guarantee for a data center facility lease, “you’d still face the question of ‘how to finance the GPUs’”; but if NVIDIA commits to purchasing any unsold compute capacity within the facility, “the GPU financing issue is resolved—and so is the data center’s financing problem.”\\nIn other words, NVIDIA’s backstop commitment effectively serves as a credit enhancement tool, enabling emerging cloud service providers—who would otherwise struggle to obtain bank loans—to mobilize significantly larger amounts of capital and accelerate data center construction.\\nStrategic Intent: Breaking the Dominance of Large Customers\\nNVIDIA’s launch of these initiatives is grounded in a clear strategic context. Currently, a small number of large cloud service providers—including Amazon, Microsoft, SpaceX, Oracle, Meta, and Google—purchase the majority of NVIDIA’s chip output. However, several of these companies are developing their own competitive AI chips, posing a potential threat to NVIDIA.\\nTo reduce its reliance on these giant clients, NVIDIA has spent the past few years actively supporting a cohort of emerging GPU cloud service providers, such as CoreWeave. The newly announced 'AI Compute Partnership Program' represents a continuation and deepening of this strategy.\\nAccording to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power, and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\\nCapital Commitments: From Equity Investments to Capacity Guarantees\\nNVIDIA’s capital commitments in this direction have already become substantial.\\nTo date, NVIDIA has invested several billion dollars in equity stakes in multiple emerging cloud service providers and, in certain cases, agreed to lease back chips from these companies—including CoreWeave and Lambda—in transactions totaling several billion dollars. According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA’s own researchers have also used GPU servers leased back from Lambda.\\nOn the capacity guarantee front, NVIDIA began advancing related deals in the autumn of last year. In September 2024, NVIDIA committed to purchasing all of CoreWeave’s unsold capacity through 2032 if CoreWeave failed to secure tenants, under a contract then valued at $6.3 billion. This move effectively alleviated investor concerns about CoreWeave’s highly leveraged business model and drove its stock price up nearly 30% within the following week.\\nAccording to a regulatory filing submitted by NVIDIA in May of this year (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\\nTaken together, NVIDIA is constructing a multi-layered mechanism to align interests: equity investments, capacity leasebacks, lease guarantees, and now revenue-sharing arrangements. Each layer deepens the financial ties between NVIDIA and downstream cloud service providers, enabling NVIDIA to directly share in the incremental returns from the commercialization of AI computing power beyond chip sales alone.\"\n  },\n  \"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\": {\n   \"object\": \"NVDA\",\n   \"kind\": \"asset\",\n   \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a guarantee or backstop for OpenAI's lease of the Ohio data center by 2027-12-31?\",\n   \"year\": \"2027\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-12-31\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"position\": 1,\n     \"label\": \"Yes\",\n     \"condition\": \"NVIDIA signed a guarantee or backstop for OpenAI's lease of the Ohio data center on or before 2027-12-31.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"position\": 2,\n     \"label\": \"No\",\n     \"condition\": \"NVIDIA signed no such guarantee or backstop on or before 2027-12-31.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"other_fields\": {\n   \"not_judged\": [\n    {\n     \"part\": \"the amount of the guarantee\",\n     \"why\": \"no amount exists until a guarantee is signed; the outcome tracks the signing\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reference\": null,\n   \"favourable_end\": null,\n   \"due_day_basis\": \"stated\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 2,\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\",\n   \"period\": \"Check date of 31 December 2027 (18 months from the 1 July 2026 item date): whether the guarantee is signed by then; set by the rule for an unsigned obligation (a check date within 18 months of the item).\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's 8-K/10-Q/10-K filings disclosing guarantees and commitments, or an official NVIDIA confirmation / the definitive agreement being announced as executed.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-1 tracks the aggregate outstanding credit support extended under the revenue-sharing 'AI Compute Partnership' model as disclosed at the next annual report; the OpenAI Ohio lease guarantee is a distinct, much larger, specific obligation still under negotiation that may never be signed and may sit outside that revenue-sharing program. NVIDIA could report a large clean-1 aggregate from other cloud partners without ever signing the OpenAI guarantee, or sign the OpenAI guarantee while accounting for it separately, so the two answers can move apart.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-01\",\n   \"publisher\": \"moomoo.com\",\n   \"title\": \"NVIDIA is about to start taking a share of cloud providers' revenue.\",\n   \"text\": \"It has been reported that, in an effort to break the monopoly of tech giants, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees for emerging cloud providers by offering GPU computing capacity lease-back arrangements to address their financing challenges. In exchange, NVIDIA will take a revenue share from these cloud providers, directly participating in profit distribution in the downstream computing market and extending its control further down the industrial chain.\\nNVIDIA is leveraging its strong balance sheet to gain market leverage, quietly evolving from a chip vendor into the 'central bank' of the AI computing ecosystem by offering financial backing to emerging cloud service providers in exchange for a share of their revenue.\\nOn July 1, according to the technology news outlet The Information, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs—if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price.\\nIn return, NVIDIA will receive a percentage of these cloud providers’ revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate gradually declining over the term of the contract. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement.\\nOn July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and allowing them to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers, and corporate clients.\\nAccording to the report, the initiative is internally referred to by some NVIDIA personnel as the 'AI Compute Partnership.' A NVIDIA spokesperson also confirmed the existence of the program. This move marks a significant strategic shift for NVIDIA:\\nOn one hand, it lowers financing barriers for emerging cloud service providers to expand its customer base; on the other, it directly participates in profit distribution within the downstream computing market through revenue sharing, thereby extending its control further down the AI value chain.\\nBusiness Model Shift: From Selling Chips to Sharing Cloud Revenue\\nAccording to NVIDIA’s official press release, the company will earn not only standard product revenue but also an additional share of cloud service revenue, creating a usage-based, recurring revenue stream. The core objective of this model is to overcome the long-standing financing barrier that has constrained startups from accessing large-scale computing power.\\nNVIDIA positions this framework as the 'DSX AI Factory' model, targeting AI service scenarios requiring continuous cross-regional operations, high utilization rates, and multi-tenant accelerated computing.\\nSharon AI and Firmus are the first cloud providers participating in this model. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs. These deployments directly reflect NVIDIA’s latest progress in transforming computing demand into deployable, financeable infrastructure.\\nNVIDIA noted that emerging AI companies have historically faced significant constraints in accessing capital-intensive infrastructure—even long-term contractual commitments have often been insufficient to secure financing for compute capacity procurement. This means that numerous AI-native firms, model developers, and inference service providers face lengthy delays when scaling their computing capabilities: site selection, power procurement, construction, and hardware commissioning—each step can take several months or even longer.\\nThe promise of this new model is to realign economic structures so these groups can rapidly access full-stack accelerated computing capabilities without waiting for the traditional infrastructure build-out cycle to complete.\\nUnderlying Rationale: Addressing the Core Challenge of GPU Financing\\nReportedly, GPUs typically represent the most expensive component in AI data centers. For chip buyers with lower credit ratings, securing sufficient loans is itself a major hurdle.\\nA data center executive commented on such NVIDIA deals as “killing two birds with one stone.” He explained that if NVIDIA merely provided a guarantee for a data center facility lease, “you’d still face the question of ‘how to finance the GPUs’”; but if NVIDIA commits to purchasing any unsold compute capacity within the facility, “the GPU financing issue is resolved—and so is the data center’s financing problem.”\\nIn other words, NVIDIA’s backstop commitment effectively serves as a credit enhancement tool, enabling emerging cloud service providers—who would otherwise struggle to obtain bank loans—to mobilize significantly larger amounts of capital and accelerate data center construction.\\nStrategic Intent: Breaking the Dominance of Large Customers\\nNVIDIA’s launch of these initiatives is grounded in a clear strategic context. Currently, a small number of large cloud service providers—including Amazon, Microsoft, SpaceX, Oracle, Meta, and Google—purchase the majority of NVIDIA’s chip output. However, several of these companies are developing their own competitive AI chips, posing a potential threat to NVIDIA.\\nTo reduce its reliance on these giant clients, NVIDIA has spent the past few years actively supporting a cohort of emerging GPU cloud service providers, such as CoreWeave. The newly announced 'AI Compute Partnership Program' represents a continuation and deepening of this strategy.\\nAccording to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power, and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\\nCapital Commitments: From Equity Investments to Capacity Guarantees\\nNVIDIA’s capital commitments in this direction have already become substantial.\\nTo date, NVIDIA has invested several billion dollars in equity stakes in multiple emerging cloud service providers and, in certain cases, agreed to lease back chips from these companies—including CoreWeave and Lambda—in transactions totaling several billion dollars. According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA’s own researchers have also used GPU servers leased back from Lambda.\\nOn the capacity guarantee front, NVIDIA began advancing related deals in the autumn of last year. In September 2024, NVIDIA committed to purchasing all of CoreWeave’s unsold capacity through 2032 if CoreWeave failed to secure tenants, under a contract then valued at $6.3 billion. This move effectively alleviated investor concerns about CoreWeave’s highly leveraged business model and drove its stock price up nearly 30% within the following week.\\nAccording to a regulatory filing submitted by NVIDIA in May of this year (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\\nTaken together, NVIDIA is constructing a multi-layered mechanism to align interests: equity investments, capacity leasebacks, lease guarantees, and now revenue-sharing arrangements. Each layer deepens the financial ties between NVIDIA and downstream cloud service providers, enabling NVIDIA to directly share in the incremental returns from the commercialization of AI computing power beyond chip sales alone.\"\n  },\n  \"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\": {\n   \"object\": \"NVDA\",\n   \"kind\": \"asset\",\n   \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a guarantee or backstop for OpenAI's lease of the Ohio data center by 2027-12-31?\",\n   \"year\": \"2027\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-12-31\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"position\": 1,\n     \"label\": \"Yes\",\n     \"condition\": \"NVIDIA signed a guarantee or backstop for OpenAI's lease of the Ohio data center on or before 2027-12-31.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"position\": 2,\n     \"label\": \"No\",\n     \"condition\": \"NVIDIA signed no such guarantee or backstop on or before 2027-12-31.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"other_fields\": {\n   \"not_judged\": [\n    {\n     \"part\": \"the amount of the guarantee\",\n     \"why\": \"no amount exists until a guarantee is signed; the outcome tracks the signing\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"part\": \"whether the guarantee is large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk\",\n     \"why\": \"a condition on the size of an obligation not yet signed; it cannot be checked before the signing\"\n    }\n   ],\n   \"reference\": null,\n   \"favourable_end\": null,\n   \"due_day_basis\": \"stated\"\n  }\n },\n {\n  \"item\": 3,\n  \"thesis\": {\n   \"description\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee/backstop for OpenAI's lease of a large data center in Ohio — and at what amount — large enough to change NVIDIA's credit risk?\",\n   \"outcome\": \"Whether NVIDIA executes (signs) a guarantee or capacity/lease backstop for OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease, and the resulting amount, by the check date.\",\n   \"period\": \"Check date of 31 December 2027 (18 months from the 1 July 2026 item date): whether the guarantee is signed by then; set by the rule for an unsigned obligation (a check date within 18 months of the item).\",\n   \"opens_as\": \"binding_obligation\",\n   \"settled_by\": \"NVIDIA's 8-K/10-Q/10-K filings disclosing guarantees and commitments, or an official NVIDIA confirmation / the definitive agreement being announced as executed.\",\n   \"why_a_separate_bet\": \"clean-1 tracks the aggregate outstanding credit support extended under the revenue-sharing 'AI Compute Partnership' model as disclosed at the next annual report; the OpenAI Ohio lease guarantee is a distinct, much larger, specific obligation still under negotiation that may never be signed and may sit outside that revenue-sharing program. NVIDIA could report a large clean-1 aggregate from other cloud partners without ever signing the OpenAI guarantee, or sign the OpenAI guarantee while accounting for it separately, so the two answers can move apart.\"\n  },\n  \"article\": {\n   \"publication_date\": \"2026-07-01\",\n   \"publisher\": \"moomoo.com\",\n   \"title\": \"NVIDIA is about to start taking a share of cloud providers' revenue.\",\n   \"text\": \"It has been reported that, in an effort to break the monopoly of tech giants, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees for emerging cloud providers by offering GPU computing capacity lease-back arrangements to address their financing challenges. In exchange, NVIDIA will take a revenue share from these cloud providers, directly participating in profit distribution in the downstream computing market and extending its control further down the industrial chain.\\nNVIDIA is leveraging its strong balance sheet to gain market leverage, quietly evolving from a chip vendor into the 'central bank' of the AI computing ecosystem by offering financial backing to emerging cloud service providers in exchange for a share of their revenue.\\nOn July 1, according to the technology news outlet The Information, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs—if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price.\\nIn return, NVIDIA will receive a percentage of these cloud providers’ revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate gradually declining over the term of the contract. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement.\\nOn July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and allowing them to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers, and corporate clients.\\nAccording to the report, the initiative is internally referred to by some NVIDIA personnel as the 'AI Compute Partnership.' A NVIDIA spokesperson also confirmed the existence of the program. This move marks a significant strategic shift for NVIDIA:\\nOn one hand, it lowers financing barriers for emerging cloud service providers to expand its customer base; on the other, it directly participates in profit distribution within the downstream computing market through revenue sharing, thereby extending its control further down the AI value chain.\\nBusiness Model Shift: From Selling Chips to Sharing Cloud Revenue\\nAccording to NVIDIA’s official press release, the company will earn not only standard product revenue but also an additional share of cloud service revenue, creating a usage-based, recurring revenue stream. The core objective of this model is to overcome the long-standing financing barrier that has constrained startups from accessing large-scale computing power.\\nNVIDIA positions this framework as the 'DSX AI Factory' model, targeting AI service scenarios requiring continuous cross-regional operations, high utilization rates, and multi-tenant accelerated computing.\\nSharon AI and Firmus are the first cloud providers participating in this model. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs. These deployments directly reflect NVIDIA’s latest progress in transforming computing demand into deployable, financeable infrastructure.\\nNVIDIA noted that emerging AI companies have historically faced significant constraints in accessing capital-intensive infrastructure—even long-term contractual commitments have often been insufficient to secure financing for compute capacity procurement. This means that numerous AI-native firms, model developers, and inference service providers face lengthy delays when scaling their computing capabilities: site selection, power procurement, construction, and hardware commissioning—each step can take several months or even longer.\\nThe promise of this new model is to realign economic structures so these groups can rapidly access full-stack accelerated computing capabilities without waiting for the traditional infrastructure build-out cycle to complete.\\nUnderlying Rationale: Addressing the Core Challenge of GPU Financing\\nReportedly, GPUs typically represent the most expensive component in AI data centers. For chip buyers with lower credit ratings, securing sufficient loans is itself a major hurdle.\\nA data center executive commented on such NVIDIA deals as “killing two birds with one stone.” He explained that if NVIDIA merely provided a guarantee for a data center facility lease, “you’d still face the question of ‘how to finance the GPUs’”; but if NVIDIA commits to purchasing any unsold compute capacity within the facility, “the GPU financing issue is resolved—and so is the data center’s financing problem.”\\nIn other words, NVIDIA’s backstop commitment effectively serves as a credit enhancement tool, enabling emerging cloud service providers—who would otherwise struggle to obtain bank loans—to mobilize significantly larger amounts of capital and accelerate data center construction.\\nStrategic Intent: Breaking the Dominance of Large Customers\\nNVIDIA’s launch of these initiatives is grounded in a clear strategic context. Currently, a small number of large cloud service providers—including Amazon, Microsoft, SpaceX, Oracle, Meta, and Google—purchase the majority of NVIDIA’s chip output. However, several of these companies are developing their own competitive AI chips, posing a potential threat to NVIDIA.\\nTo reduce its reliance on these giant clients, NVIDIA has spent the past few years actively supporting a cohort of emerging GPU cloud service providers, such as CoreWeave. The newly announced 'AI Compute Partnership Program' represents a continuation and deepening of this strategy.\\nAccording to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power, and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\\nCapital Commitments: From Equity Investments to Capacity Guarantees\\nNVIDIA’s capital commitments in this direction have already become substantial.\\nTo date, NVIDIA has invested several billion dollars in equity stakes in multiple emerging cloud service providers and, in certain cases, agreed to lease back chips from these companies—including CoreWeave and Lambda—in transactions totaling several billion dollars. According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA’s own researchers have also used GPU servers leased back from Lambda.\\nOn the capacity guarantee front, NVIDIA began advancing related deals in the autumn of last year. In September 2024, NVIDIA committed to purchasing all of CoreWeave’s unsold capacity through 2032 if CoreWeave failed to secure tenants, under a contract then valued at $6.3 billion. This move effectively alleviated investor concerns about CoreWeave’s highly leveraged business model and drove its stock price up nearly 30% within the following week.\\nAccording to a regulatory filing submitted by NVIDIA in May of this year (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\\nTaken together, NVIDIA is constructing a multi-layered mechanism to align interests: equity investments, capacity leasebacks, lease guarantees, and now revenue-sharing arrangements. Each layer deepens the financial ties between NVIDIA and downstream cloud service providers, enabling NVIDIA to directly share in the incremental returns from the commercialization of AI computing power beyond chip sales alone.\"\n  },\n  \"ladder_as_the_judge_is_shown_it\": {\n   \"object\": \"NVDA\",\n   \"kind\": \"asset\",\n   \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a guarantee or backstop for OpenAI's lease of the Ohio data center by 2027-12-31?\",\n   \"year\": \"2027\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-12-31\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"position\": 1,\n     \"label\": \"Yes\",\n     \"condition\": \"NVIDIA signed a guarantee or backstop for OpenAI's lease of the Ohio data center on or before 2027-12-31.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"position\": 2,\n     \"label\": \"No\",\n     \"condition\": \"NVIDIA signed no such guarantee or backstop on or before 2027-12-31.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"other_fields\": {\n   \"not_judged\": [],\n   \"reference\": null,\n   \"favourable_end\": null,\n   \"due_day_basis\": \"stated\"\n  }\n }\n]","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
